  set:  # dd14
    description: "[REVIEW 2026-11-01, CURRENT] 2026-11-01 review at dev 100b6fbb6e: analyze_java on the filed A/B fixture emits A.m -> A.m (ast_call_direct); java.py L3021 still routes object_node 'super' through Case 1 (gikuk 495f9747f5 made the lookup positional, behaviour unchanged). In survey output the self-loop is removed by deduplicate_edges(remove_self_loops=True), which is why WI-latin saw no edge. A visible false edge exists too: super.go() inside a NON-override Kid.other binds Kid.other -> Kid.go instead of Base.go; super.write(b) on external OutputStream also self-loops. (Original text below; the review entry in the thread has the evidence.)\n\nFOUND 2026-09-30 while working WI-halin (branch jgstern-agent/fix/halin). java.py's Case 1 (the current-class lookup) handles super.m() exactly like this.m(): it looks up <EnclosingClass>.m, so a super call inside an override resolves to the OVERRIDE ITSELF (a false self-recursion edge) rather than to the parent's method.\n\nREPRO (dev 8cfcab7b07 and the halin branch alike; analyze_java on one file):\n  public class A extends B { void m() { super.m(); } }\n  class B { void m() {} }\n-> one calls edge, A.m -> A.m (ast_call_direct, resolved). Expected: A.m -> B.m, or an unresolved edge carrying enclosing_class for the inherited_calls linker to walk from the PARENT.\n\nWI-halin deliberately left super.m() on its old path (it narrowed Case 1 to object_node None / this / super, excluding receiver expressions only), so this behaviour is unchanged there. Corpus frequency not measured. Direction: a false edge on the call graph (slice / reverse slice / centrality), and it can hide the parent's body from reachability. Siblings (kotlin/scala/csharp super calls) not checked."  # dd14
